<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">North Sea Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1016 CE – 1035 CE · capital Winchester</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived union of England, Denmark and Norway under Cnut the Great, which made the North Sea the centre of an Anglo-Scandinavian realm.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a>, son of the Danish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1016 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a>. He inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from his brother Harald around 1018 and, after driving out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1028, also claiming lordship over part of the Swedes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a> ruled mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, often from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, keeping English institutions, issuing law codes and marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>, widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a>. He governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> through regents, including his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> and the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lade-trondheim">Lade</a>. A pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1027, where he attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II</a>'s coronation, raised his standing among Christian rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a> died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a> in 1035, and his realm broke apart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> rejected Danish rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus the Good</a>, while his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. After Harthacnut's death in 1042 the English throne returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a> went their own ways.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-sea-empire.jpg" alt="North Sea Empire" width="320" height="255"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cnut_lands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1016 CE – 1035 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Winchester</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cnut the Great</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English, Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Denmark, United Kingdom, Norway, Sweden</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anglo-Scandinavian Empire, Empire of Cnut</dd></dl></aside></div>

